{:edb/db-before-t 1506 :edb/db-after-t 1507 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534819 50 #inst "2026-10-08T08:01:12.266Z" 13194139534819 true] [17592186119574 1100 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$  and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle.$" 13194139534819 true] [17592186119574 1100 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,5)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle$." 13194139534819 false] [17592186119574 1145 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+ s\\mathbf{b}+ t \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,5 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= ⟨ - 3, 2, 5⟩ + s⟨17, 3, 15⟩ + t⟨5, 0,-8⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534819 false] [17592186119574 1145 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+ s\\mathbf{b}+ t \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,5 \\rangle,$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-3,2,5⟩+s\\,⟨17,3,15⟩+t\\,⟨5,0,-8⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534819 true] [17592186119575 1145 "The direction vectors of the lines are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -1,7,2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 2\\sqrt{3},9,-8 \\rangle\n$$\n\n, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $C(2,50,-19)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 2,50,-19 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= c + sv_{1} + tv_{2} \\\\\nr &= ⟨2, 50,-19⟩ + s⟨ - 1, 7, 2⟩ + t⟨2\\sqrt{3}, 9,-8⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534819 false] [17592186119575 1145 "The direction vectors of the lines are $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle  -1,7,2  \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 2\\sqrt{3},9,-8 \\rangle$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $C(2,50,-19)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 2,50,-19 \\rangle.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =c+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨2,50,-19⟩+s\\,⟨-1,7,2⟩+t\\,⟨2\\sqrt{3},9,-8⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534819 true] [17592186119576 1100 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(-1,4,-3)$, $B(7,5,4)$, and $C(2,-2,8)$." 13194139534819 false] [17592186119576 1100 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(-1,4,-3),$ $B(7,5,4),$ and $C(2,-2,8).$" 13194139534819 true] [17592186119576 1145 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= ⟨7, 5, 4⟩ - ⟨ - 1, 4,-3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨8, 1, 7⟩ \\\\\nAC^(→) &= ⟨2,-2, 8⟩ - ⟨ - 1, 4,-3⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3,-6, 11⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -1,4,-3 \\rangle\n$$\n\nof the point $A$, then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= a + sAB^(→) + tAC^(→) \\\\\nr &= ⟨ - 1, 4,-3⟩ + s⟨8, 1, 7⟩ + t⟨3,-6, 11⟩, s, t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The form of the final equation depends on our choice of vectors and the point on the plane. We could have picked other vectors. Likewise, we could have chosen $B$ or $C$ instead of $A$ as our point on the plane. So, the answer is *not unique*!" 13194139534819 false] [17592186119576 1145 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨7,5,4⟩-⟨-1,4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,1,7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨2,-2,8⟩-⟨-1,4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -1,4,-3 \\rangle$ of the point $A,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s\\overset{\\to }{AB}+t\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\ r & =⟨-1,4,-3⟩+s⟨8,1,7⟩+t⟨3,-6,11⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The form of the final equation depends on our choice of vectors and the point on the plane. We could have picked other vectors. Likewise, we could have chosen $B$ or $C$ instead of $A$ as our point on the plane. So, the answer is *not unique*!" 13194139534819 true] [17592186119588 1100 "Find a normal vector to the plane\n$\\mathbf{r}= \\langle -6,3, 2 \\rangle + t \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle + s \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle,\\qquad t,\\,s \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139534819 false] [17592186119588 1100 "Find a normal vector to the plane \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}= \\langle -6,3, 2 \\rangle + t \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle + s \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle,\\qquad t,\\,s \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139534819 true] [17592186119588 1145 "From the parametric equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{w} = \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nare parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= v \\cdot w \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= i\\begin{vmatrix}j & k \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}- 1 \\\\\n&= 4 \\mid - 3 \\mid 2 \\\\\n&= i - 12j - 20k \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-12,-20⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 1, -12, -20 \\rangle\n$$" 13194139534819 false] [17592186119588 1145 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{w} = \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-12j-20k \\\\  & =⟨1,-12,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 1, -12, -20 \\rangle.$" 13194139534819 true] [17592186119854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312062 13194139534819 true] [17592186119854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312069 13194139534819 true] [17592186119854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312111 13194139534819 true] [17592186119854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312153 13194139534819 true] [17592186119854 1020 #edb/ref 17592186312160 13194139534819 true] [17592186119855 